WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.015
Lesson 5 becomes much stronger when the learner stops imagining magnetism as a forward shove and starts reading it as a sideways steering effect.

2
00:00:05.015 --> 00:00:10.031
Geometry means perpendicular force.

3
00:00:10.031 --> 00:00:15.046
Magnetic force sits at right angles to both the field and the moving charge or current.

4
00:00:15.046 --> 00:00:20.061
Angle factor means crossing matters.

5
00:00:20.061 --> 00:00:25.077
Only the component that crosses the field contributes to the force.

6
00:00:25.077 --> 00:00:30.092
Direction flips means reverse one vector.

7
00:00:30.092 --> 00:00:35.107
Changing the current, velocity, or field direction flips the force direction.

8
00:00:35.107 --> 00:00:40.123
The weak move is to say the magnetic field pushes along the field lines, which erases the sideways geometry that defines the topic.

9
00:00:40.123 --> 00:00:45.138
The worked example becomes easier because the angle factor and the direction rule now feel physically motivated.

10
00:00:45.138 --> 00:00:50.153
Magnetic force is a crossing-angle story, not a forward-push story.

11
00:00:50.153 --> 00:00:55.169
Keep this takeaway in view: Keep the sideways geometry visible from the first slide.

12
00:00:55.169 --> 00:01:00.184
Geometry means perpendicular force.

13
00:01:00.184 --> 00:01:05.199
This is the core idea.

14
00:01:05.199 --> 00:01:10.215
Angle factor means crossing matters.

15
00:01:10.215 --> 00:01:15.230
Parallel motion gives no force.

16
00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:19.578
This diagram matters because it keeps the three directions separate.

17
00:01:19.578 --> 00:01:23.925
The field direction is one vector, the current or particle motion is another, and the force is the sideways result.

18
00:01:23.925 --> 00:01:28.273
Field means reference direction.

19
00:01:28.273 --> 00:01:32.620
This is the magnetic environment the charge or wire is crossing.

20
00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:36.968
Motion or current means crossing direction.

21
00:01:36.968 --> 00:01:41.315
This decides whether the force is zero, partial, or maximum.

22
00:01:41.315 --> 00:01:45.663
Force means sideways result.

23
00:01:45.663 --> 00:01:50.010
The magnetic response arrives perpendicular to both of the other directions.

24
00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:54.358
Without the diagram, students often remember the formula but forget what angle theta is actually measuring.

25
00:01:54.358 --> 00:01:58.705
The worked example becomes safer because the angle can now be read from geometry instead of guessed from memory.

26
00:01:58.705 --> 00:02:03.053
Use the diagram to identify the crossing angle before substitution.

27
00:02:03.053 --> 00:02:07.400
Keep this takeaway in view: Three directions must stay distinct on the board.

28
00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:11.748
Field means reference direction.

29
00:02:11.748 --> 00:02:16.095
The magnetic field sets one axis of the geometry.

30
00:02:16.095 --> 00:02:20.442
Motion or current means crossing direction.

31
00:02:20.442 --> 00:02:24.790
This creates the angle factor.

32
00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:29.168
A crosswind analogy helps because it separates motion along the wind from motion across the wind.

33
00:02:29.168 --> 00:02:33.546
Crossing creates the sideways shove; moving parallel does not.

34
00:02:33.546 --> 00:02:37.924
Parallel travel means no side-kick.

35
00:02:37.924 --> 00:02:42.302
If you move with the field direction, there is no crossing component.

36
00:02:42.302 --> 00:02:46.681
Perpendicular travel means strongest side-kick.

37
00:02:46.681 --> 00:02:51.059
A full crossing gives the largest sideways effect.

38
00:02:51.059 --> 00:02:55.437
Intermediate angle means partial side-kick.

39
00:02:55.437 --> 00:02:59.815
Only part of the motion is crossing the field.

40
00:02:59.815 --> 00:03:04.193
The weak analogy mistake is to think speed alone decides the force, when geometry and angle matter just as much.

41
00:03:04.193 --> 00:03:08.571
The worked example becomes easier because the sine factor now has a physical meaning instead of being a memorized decoration.

42
00:03:08.571 --> 00:03:12.949
Crossing component first, full speed second.

43
00:03:12.949 --> 00:03:17.328
Keep this takeaway in view: The sine factor is the crossing component in disguise.

44
00:03:17.328 --> 00:03:21.706
Parallel travel means no side-kick.

45
00:03:21.706 --> 00:03:26.084
No crossing means no magnetic force.

46
00:03:26.084 --> 00:03:30.462
Perpendicular travel means strongest side-kick.

47
00:03:30.462 --> 00:03:34.840
Full crossing gives maximum force.

48
00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:39.751
The formula board is much easier to remember when both relations are read as the same crossing-angle story in two different contexts.

49
00:03:39.751 --> 00:03:44.662
Charge case means use q and v.

50
00:03:44.662 --> 00:03:49.573
A moving charged particle feels the sideways magnetic force.

51
00:03:49.573 --> 00:03:54.484
Wire case means use I and L.

52
00:03:54.484 --> 00:03:59.396
A current-carrying conductor feels the same geometry through the active length.

53
00:03:59.396 --> 00:04:04.307
Shared idea means crossing component.

54
00:04:04.307 --> 00:04:09.218
The sine factor tells you how much of the motion or current cuts across the field.

55
00:04:09.218 --> 00:04:14.129
Keep these relations visible: F = Bqv sin(theta); F = BIL sin(theta); and theta measures the angle between the field and the motion or current direction.

56
00:04:14.129 --> 00:04:19.040
Parallel alignment gives zero force.

57
00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:23.951
Ninety degrees gives the strongest case because the full motion or current crosses the field.

58
00:04:23.951 --> 00:04:28.862
A common error is to use the wrong angle or forget that sin zero is what makes the parallel case force-free.

59
00:04:28.862 --> 00:04:33.773
The worked example becomes cleaner because the learner sees one geometry idea wearing two algebraic outfits.

60
00:04:33.773 --> 00:04:38.684
One angle story explains both formulas.

61
00:04:38.684 --> 00:04:43.596
Keep this takeaway in view: Use the angle meaning before you substitute values.

62
00:04:43.596 --> 00:04:48.507
Charge case means use q and v.

63
00:04:48.507 --> 00:04:53.418
This is the particle version.

64
00:04:53.418 --> 00:04:58.329
Wire case means use I and L.

65
00:04:58.329 --> 00:05:03.240
This is the motor-effect wire version.

66
00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:08.012
A dependable Lesson 5 routine begins by deciding whether the question is about a moving charge or a wire, then finding the angle, then deciding the sideways direction, and only then calculating the magnitude.

67
00:05:08.012 --> 00:05:12.784
Step 1 means pick the context.

68
00:05:12.784 --> 00:05:17.556
Choose the charge formula or the wire formula.

69
00:05:17.556 --> 00:05:22.328
Step 2 means read theta carefully.

70
00:05:22.328 --> 00:05:27.100
Theta is between the field and the motion or current.

71
00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:31.872
Step 3 means decide the side-kick direction.

72
00:05:31.872 --> 00:05:36.644
Use reversal logic before you calculate.

73
00:05:36.644 --> 00:05:41.416
If the angle is guessed after substitution, students often mix up sine and cosine thinking without even noticing.

74
00:05:41.416 --> 00:05:46.188
The worked example becomes steadier because the geometry has already been decoded before the arithmetic starts.

75
00:05:46.188 --> 00:05:50.960
Decode the geometry first and calculate second.

76
00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:55.732
Keep this takeaway in view: Let the geometry speak before the calculator does.

77
00:05:55.732 --> 00:06:00.504
Step 1 means pick the context.

78
00:06:00.504 --> 00:06:05.276
Charge or wire?

79
00:06:05.276 --> 00:06:10.048
Step 2 means read theta carefully.

80
00:06:10.048 --> 00:06:14.820
Theta is a geometric angle.

81
00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:18.695
When the motion or current runs parallel to the field, nothing cuts across the field direction.

82
00:06:18.695 --> 00:06:22.570
That means the sine factor is zero and the magnetic force vanishes.

83
00:06:22.570 --> 00:06:26.445
Parallel geometry means no crossing.

84
00:06:26.445 --> 00:06:30.320
The motion is aligned with the field.

85
00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:34.195
Angle result means sin zero = 0.

86
00:06:34.195 --> 00:06:38.070
The formula matches the geometry.

87
00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:41.945
Lesson gain means zero force case.

88
00:06:41.945 --> 00:06:45.820
This is a strong reasonableness check.

89
00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:49.695
The weak statement is that a moving charge must always feel some magnetic force if a field is present, but the geometry says otherwise.

90
00:06:49.695 --> 00:06:53.570
The worked example becomes easier because zero-force cases can be recognized before calculation.

91
00:06:53.570 --> 00:06:57.445
Field presence alone is not enough; crossing geometry is required.

92
00:06:57.445 --> 00:07:01.320
Keep this takeaway in view: Magnetic force needs a crossing component.

93
00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:05.195
Parallel geometry means no crossing.

94
00:07:05.195 --> 00:07:09.070
Aligned motion gives no side-kick.

95
00:07:09.070 --> 00:07:12.945
Angle result means sin zero = 0.

96
00:07:12.945 --> 00:07:16.820
The formula confirms the picture.

97
00:07:16.820 --> 00:07:20.901
At ninety degrees, the crossing is complete.

98
00:07:20.901 --> 00:07:24.981
That means the sine factor is one and the magnetic force reaches its largest value for the chosen B, q, v or B, I, L values.

99
00:07:24.981 --> 00:07:29.062
Perpendicular geometry means full crossing.

100
00:07:29.062 --> 00:07:33.142
Every bit of the motion or current cuts across the field.

101
00:07:33.142 --> 00:07:37.223
Angle result means sin ninety = 1.

102
00:07:37.223 --> 00:07:41.304
The formula reaches its full-size case.

103
00:07:41.304 --> 00:07:45.384
Lesson gain means benchmark case.

104
00:07:45.384 --> 00:07:49.465
This makes it easier to judge partial-angle answers.

105
00:07:49.465 --> 00:07:53.546
A weak answer treats ninety degrees as just another angle instead of the clean maximum case.

106
00:07:53.546 --> 00:07:57.626
The worked example becomes easier because the learner can compare any partial-angle answer to the maximum benchmark.

107
00:07:57.626 --> 00:08:01.707
Use the right-angle case as your force ceiling.

108
00:08:01.707 --> 00:08:05.788
Keep this takeaway in view: Right-angle crossing sets the strongest case.

109
00:08:05.788 --> 00:08:09.868
Perpendicular geometry means full crossing.

110
00:08:09.868 --> 00:08:13.949
This is the largest usable component.

111
00:08:13.949 --> 00:08:18.029
Angle result means sin ninety = 1.

112
00:08:18.029 --> 00:08:22.110
The formula is at full strength.

113
00:08:22.110 --> 00:08:26.289
The sideways force depends on the geometry of the vectors.

114
00:08:26.289 --> 00:08:30.469
Reverse one of the participating directions and the sideways result flips as well.

115
00:08:30.469 --> 00:08:34.648
Reverse B means force flips.

116
00:08:34.648 --> 00:08:38.828
Changing the field direction changes the sideways outcome.

117
00:08:38.828 --> 00:08:43.007
Reverse v or I means force flips.

118
00:08:43.007 --> 00:08:47.186
Changing the motion or current direction does the same.

119
00:08:47.186 --> 00:08:51.366
Lesson gain means magnitude can stay the same.

120
00:08:51.366 --> 00:08:55.545
Direction may change even when the force size does not.

121
00:08:55.545 --> 00:08:59.724
A weak answer says the force gets bigger or smaller when only the direction has changed.

122
00:08:59.724 --> 00:09:03.904
This makes the worked example safer whenever the question asks for direction rather than just magnitude.

123
00:09:03.904 --> 00:09:08.083
Vector reversal changes the side-kick direction first.

124
00:09:08.083 --> 00:09:12.262
Keep this takeaway in view: Do not confuse direction change with magnitude change.

125
00:09:12.262 --> 00:09:16.442
Reverse B means force flips.

126
00:09:16.442 --> 00:09:20.621
The sideways direction changes.

127
00:09:20.621 --> 00:09:24.801
Reverse v or I means force flips.

128
00:09:24.801 --> 00:09:28.980
The same reversal logic applies.

129
00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:33.229
Because the force is perpendicular to the motion, magnetic force changes direction very naturally.

130
00:09:33.229 --> 00:09:37.478
It is better to think of it as steering the motion than pushing it forward along the field.

131
00:09:37.478 --> 00:09:41.726
Perpendicular force means sideways effect.

132
00:09:41.726 --> 00:09:45.975
The force acts across the motion, not along it.

133
00:09:45.975 --> 00:09:50.224
Motion result means turning tendency.

134
00:09:50.224 --> 00:09:54.472
A sideways force naturally bends the path.

135
00:09:54.472 --> 00:09:58.721
Lesson gain means bridge to orbits and motors.

136
00:09:58.721 --> 00:10:02.970
This is the same geometry the next lesson will deepen.

137
00:10:02.970 --> 00:10:07.219
The weak story says the magnet speeds the charge up in the field direction, which erases the key sideways geometry.

138
00:10:07.219 --> 00:10:11.468
The worked example becomes more meaningful because the force is now seen as a direction-changing effect.

139
00:10:11.468 --> 00:10:15.716
Magnetic force is best read as steering, not forward driving.

140
00:10:15.716 --> 00:10:19.965
Keep this takeaway in view: Store the steering idea for Lesson 6.

141
00:10:19.965 --> 00:10:24.214
Perpendicular force means sideways effect.

142
00:10:24.214 --> 00:10:28.462
The force changes direction naturally.

143
00:10:28.462 --> 00:10:32.711
Motion result means turning tendency.

144
00:10:32.711 --> 00:10:36.960
This is a steering story.

145
00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:40.453
Use this checkpoint to test whether the geometry is really clear.

146
00:10:40.453 --> 00:10:43.946
Parallel motion means no crossing component, so theta is zero, sine theta is zero, and the magnetic force disappears.

147
00:10:43.946 --> 00:10:47.439
Question means parallel to the field.

148
00:10:47.439 --> 00:10:50.931
Why does the force vanish?

149
00:10:50.931 --> 00:10:54.424
Route means no crossing component.

150
00:10:54.424 --> 00:10:57.917
The geometry offers nothing to push sideways.

151
00:10:57.917 --> 00:11:01.410
Answer check means sin zero = 0.

152
00:11:01.410 --> 00:11:04.903
The formula matches the picture exactly.

153
00:11:04.903 --> 00:11:08.396
No crossing means no magnetic side-kick.

154
00:11:08.396 --> 00:11:11.889
Keep this takeaway in view: Geometry explains the zero before the formula confirms it.

155
00:11:11.889 --> 00:11:15.381
Question means parallel to the field.

156
00:11:15.381 --> 00:11:18.874
Think geometry before algebra.

157
00:11:18.874 --> 00:11:22.367
Route means no crossing component.

158
00:11:22.367 --> 00:11:25.860
That is the key physical reason.

159
00:11:25.860 --> 00:11:30.696
Carry one route into the worked example.

160
00:11:30.696 --> 00:11:35.531
Decide whether the case is charge or wire, identify the angle between the field and the motion or current, then evaluate the sine factor and calculate the force magnitude.

161
00:11:35.531 --> 00:11:40.367
Step 1 means pick formula.

162
00:11:40.367 --> 00:11:45.202
Charge case or wire case?

163
00:11:45.202 --> 00:11:50.038
Step 2 means read theta.

164
00:11:50.038 --> 00:11:54.874
Angle goes between field and motion or current.

165
00:11:54.874 --> 00:11:59.709
Step 3 means interpret geometry.

166
00:11:59.709 --> 00:12:04.545
Check whether the force is zero, partial, or maximum.

167
00:12:04.545 --> 00:12:09.381
Keep these relations visible: choose F = Bqv sin(theta) or F = BIL sin(theta); theta is between the field and the motion or current direction; and use the geometry to interpret the result.

168
00:12:09.381 --> 00:12:14.216
Parallel means zero force, ninety degrees means the maximum case, and reversed vectors flip the direction.

169
00:12:14.216 --> 00:12:19.052
The angle is not decoration; it is the heart of the example.

170
00:12:19.052 --> 00:12:23.888
Keep this takeaway in view: Take the geometry with you into the numbers.

171
00:12:23.888 --> 00:12:28.723
Step 1 means pick formula.

172
00:12:28.723 --> 00:12:33.559
Choose charge or wire first.

173
00:12:33.559 --> 00:12:38.394
Step 2 means read theta.

174
00:12:38.394 --> 00:12:43.230
Use the correct geometric angle.
